Paraphrasing the good analysis by Wuwenbin:

"
SELECT DISTINCT
      (CASE WHEN EXISTS (SELECT ref_49.medal_now FROM t1)
       THEN 1 ELSE 0
       END) IS NULL
FROM t2 LEFT JOIN t3 AS ref_49 ON TRUE;

In the current scenario, because the return result of the CASE WHEN
expression is guaranteed to be non-NULL, the
Item_func_isnull::resolve_type function clears the used_tables_cache
information that was set during the earlier subquery processing.

Join elimination logic relies on used_tables() to determine if an
inner table is referenced outside the ON clause. The used_tables() is
0. Combined with the presence of a DISTINCT clause, the conditions for
left join elimination are met, and the inner table is eliminated.

However, the correlated subquery remains. In the subsequent PTRC
processing phase, this leads to a crash because the inner table
pointer becomes a nullptr.
"

Vaguely similar problems had occurred before, but it was when the
subquery was part of the ON condition of the eliminated LEFT JOIN; so
I had solved it by calling cleanup_after_removal() on this ON
condition, so that PTRC does not meet the subquery. But here, the
"elimination" of the subquery happens in
Item_func_isnull::resolve_type() which does not care to call
cleanup_after_removal(). So PTRC is still involved. A more robust fix,
which covers both cases (and maybe other ones due to other
Item_func-s?), is to make PTRC more careful.

Once this is done in PTRC code, some comments in the code of left join
elimination are modified accordingly.
